  <h2>Before this</h2>
  <ul class="exp">
    <li><strong>Turing</strong> <span class="when">Google Gemini evaluation programme</span><br>
      Judging AI output has no single right answer, so the real test is whether two people would
      score the same response the same way. I rated Gemini answers against a written rubric,
      wrote tricky questions to find where the rubric failed, and wrote down the rules so a second
      person would agree. Eighty hours of work that taught me how to turn vague quality into
      something you can check.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How I work</h2>
  <p style="max-width:var(--content-max);color:var(--text-3);font-size:var(--text-sm);margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">
    These are not aspirational. They are extracted from what actually happened in the projects above.</p>
  <ol class="commitments">
    <li><strong>Establish the baseline before training anything.</strong> A model that cannot beat a simple guess does not deserve to exist.</li>
    <li><strong>Name the error you can afford before choosing a threshold.</strong> Rejecting a good image costs one retry; accepting a bad one sends it to the client.</li>
    <li><strong>Model the process in BPMN before writing code.</strong> Draw the process on paper before coding it. The automation should replace what actually happens, not what you think happens.</li>
    <li><strong>Freeze the preprocessing with the model</strong> so the way you prepare data in training stays the same in production.</li>
    <li><strong>Version every artefact</strong> so you can roll back a bad release instead of spending hours figuring out what broke.</li>
    <li><strong>State what breaks, in writing, next to what works.</strong> Every project page says what can go wrong before it says what works.</li>
  </ol>

